\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Spin-frame compatibility is separate from internal covariance}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The [internal gauge theorem](fdip-009P) uses
#{I_S\otimes\rho(g(x))} on field values. It does not cover an
arbitrary unitary transformation on #{S}. Already in one
coordinate, the chosen gamma matrix is
##{X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
For #{Z=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)}, one has #{ZXZ=-X}.
With trivial links and fixed lattice directions,
##{ZD_0Z=-D_0\ne D_0.}
The inequality holds for every permitted period #{N\ge3}:
the Fourier momentum #{2\pi /N} has nonzero sine. Thus even a
constant unitary change on the spin factor fails the claimed
fixed-gamma conjugacy unless the Clifford/frame data are
transformed compatibly.}

\p{A spin action instead comes with its specified vector action
and Clifford compatibility. For a constant orthogonal frame
change #{R}, the relevant relation has the form
##{\sigma(s)c(v)\sigma(s)^{-1}=c(Rv).}
An action on lattice differences must respect the same change
of directions; a varying frame needs additional transport data.
No such spin-frame transformation is part of the internal gauge
statement above. The [compact real Spin cover](fcap-0039)
uses the positive quadratic form, and its action is on the
spatial vector carrier. It is not, by its name alone, an action
on an arbitrary internal fiber.}

\p{In dimension three, the [quaternion map](fdip-007G)
#{SU(2)\to SO(3)} has kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. Its binary finite
subgroups supply useful representations, but their roles remain
distinct. For example the [faithful two-dimensional fiber](fdip-007K)
#{F=\mathbb C^2} of #{2T} can be used internally in
#{S\otimes F}; then it commutes with the gamma matrices on
#{S}. The corresponding three-dimensional rotation representation
kills the center and is different data. Neither choice identifies
the internal factor with the spatial Clifford module or provides
a lattice frame action.}
